        "content": {
          "parts": [
            {
              "text": "In the **Logan** region and along the **Western Corridor (Ipswich to Greater Springfield/Western Brisbane)**, several law firms have dedicated practices handling **estate litigation, Family Provision Applications (FPAs), and contested wills** under the *Succession Act 1981 (Qld)*.\n\n---\n\n### **Logan Region**\n\n1. **Turner Freeman Lawyers (Logan / Springwood)**\n   * **Focus Areas:** Will challenges, lack of testamentary capacity, undue influence, and Family Provision Applications. \n   * **Profile:** Turner Freeman’s Queensland succession litigation team is consistently ranked by *Doyle’s Guide* for estate litigation. They have an established Logan office and the litigation resources to take on large, complex disputed estates.\n\n2. **Springwood Lawyers (Springwood)**\n   * **Focus Areas:** Wills and estates disputes, elder law, and contentious probate.\n   * **Profile:** A boutique local practice based in Springwood focusing specifically on family, elder, and succession law, offering direct courtroom representation in contentious inheritance matters.\n\n3. **SKC Lawyers (Berrinba / Logan Central)**\n   * **Focus Areas:** Contested estates, probate disputes, and estate administration issues.\n   * **Profile:** A local suburban firm handling succession disputes and negotiations between executors and beneficiaries.\n\n---\n\n### **Western Corridor (Ipswich, Brookwater, Springfield to Brisbane)**\n\n1. **Fallu McMillan Lawyers (Ipswich)**\n   * **Focus Areas:** Estate litigation, will validity challenges, and Family Provision Applications.\n   * **Profile:** One of the most established legal practices in Ipswich. Their wills and estates division routinely acts for both executors defending the estate and disappointed beneficiaries filing FPAs.\n\n2. **Keane Legal (Ipswich & Brookwater / Greater Springfield)**\n   * **Focus Areas:** Testamentary capacity contests, Family Provision Applications, and executor removal applications.\n   * **Profile:** Conveniently located right in the Western Corridor with offices in Ipswich and Brookwater. They specifically highlight contested succession proceedings in the Brisbane Supreme and District Courts.\n\n3. **Stanford Legal (Springfield Central)**\n   * **Focus Areas:** Estate disputes, family provision claims, and challenges to will validity.\n   * **Profile:** Based in Springfield Central, serving both the Ipswich and Western Brisbane corridors, handling negotiations and court filings for contested estates.\n\n4. **Jacaranda Law (Ipswich)**\n   * **Focus Areas:** Unfair will disputes, claims by spouses/de facto partners/children, and undue influence.\n   * **Profile:** A practice dedicated exclusively to wills, probate, and contested estates, providing focused advisory on FPAs and executor disputes.\n\n5. **Richard Zande & Associates (Ipswich)**\n   * **Focus Areas:** Testamentary disputes and statutory family provision claims.\n   * **Profile:** Operating in Ipswich for over 60 years, this firm regularly advises claimants and defending executors regarding Queensland statutory inheritance rules.\n\n6. **Turner Freeman Lawyers (Ipswich Branch)**\n   * **Focus Areas:** Will disputes, moral duty claims, and contested probate.\n   * **Profile:** Similar to their Logan office, their Ipswich team provides top-tier estate litigation resources with deferred or speculative fee options for certain eligible claims.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Queensland Deadlines to Keep in Mind**\nIf you are planning to bring an FPA or contest a will in Queensland:\n* **6 Months:** You must give written notice of your intention to make a claim to the executor within **6 months** of the deceased’s date of death.\n* **9 Months:** The formal application must be filed in Court within **9 months** of the deceased’s date of death. \n\n*Tip:* Most of these firms offer an initial consultation or preliminary claim assessment (often free or fixed-fee), and several will consider acting on a **deferred payment** or **No Win, No Fee** basis if the estate is of sufficient size and the claim has strong merit.",
